1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,547
A cake recipe asks for
eight ounces of flour.

2
00:00:03,547 --> 00:00:06,175
A road sign says sixty miles to town.

3
00:00:06,175 --> 00:00:09,007
A cricket pitch is twenty two yards long.

4
00:00:09,407 --> 00:00:11,089
Ounces, miles, yards.

5
00:00:11,089 --> 00:00:13,782
None of them is a metre or a kilogram.

6
00:00:13,782 --> 00:00:15,744
So where do they come from?

7
00:00:16,194 --> 00:00:19,434
Aarav meets the same
puzzle at the cycle shop.

8
00:00:19,434 --> 00:00:21,499
His tyre says forty p s i.

9
00:00:21,849 --> 00:00:24,138
But the pump gauge only shows bar.

10
00:00:24,138 --> 00:00:26,513
Same air, same tyre, two numbers.

11
00:00:27,013 --> 00:00:28,938
By the end, you will read both.

12
00:00:29,588 --> 00:00:31,589
So what is a system of units?

13
00:00:31,939 --> 00:00:36,470
It is one complete set of base units:
one for length, one for mass, one

14
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:37,370
for time.

15
00:00:37,244 --> 00:00:39,472
Everything else is built from them.

16
00:00:39,872 --> 00:00:42,048
New base units give a new system.

17
00:00:42,448 --> 00:00:47,827
NCERT names three systems that were
used for a long time: the CGS, the

18
00:00:47,827 --> 00:00:50,678
FPS or British, and the MKS.

19
00:00:51,078 --> 00:00:52,879
Today the world uses SI.

20
00:00:52,879 --> 00:00:56,182
It grew out of MKS, with seven base units.

21
00:00:56,832 --> 00:00:59,173
Let's look at the three older systems.

22
00:00:59,523 --> 00:01:02,510
CGS stands for centimetre, gram, second.

23
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:05,521
FPS stands for foot, pound, second.

24
00:01:05,521 --> 00:01:09,577
It is the British system, still
seen in feet, miles and pounds.

25
00:01:09,977 --> 00:01:12,483
MKS: metre, kilogram, second.

26
00:01:12,883 --> 00:01:13,876
Look closely.

27
00:01:13,876 --> 00:01:15,930
All three agree on the second.

28
00:01:15,930 --> 00:01:17,828
Only length and mass differ.

29
00:01:18,478 --> 00:01:21,905
Now one motion, three systems,
on our motion notebook.

30
00:01:22,255 --> 00:01:26,717
A two kilogram trolley is pushed
with a steady force of four newtons.

31
00:01:27,117 --> 00:01:30,480
It speeds up by two metres
per second every second.

32
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:32,558
Watch the velocity graph climb.

33
00:01:32,958 --> 00:01:33,906
Slow motion.

34
00:01:33,906 --> 00:01:36,419
The orange force arrow stays the same.

35
00:01:36,419 --> 00:01:38,873
The blue velocity arrow keeps growing.

36
00:01:39,273 --> 00:01:41,555
Now switch the dial to CGS.

37
00:01:41,555 --> 00:01:44,727
The speed now reads in
centimetres per second.

38
00:01:44,727 --> 00:01:47,092
The numbers jump a hundred times.

39
00:01:47,492 --> 00:01:48,808
Switch to FPS.

40
00:01:48,808 --> 00:01:53,572
Now it is feet per second, about three
point three times the metre number.

41
00:01:53,972 --> 00:01:55,755
The trolley never changed.

42
00:01:55,755 --> 00:01:57,239
Only the system did.

43
00:01:57,889 --> 00:02:01,098
Now, how big is the force
unit in each system?

44
00:02:01,098 --> 00:02:02,579
One step at a time.

45
00:02:02,929 --> 00:02:03,833
Step one.

46
00:02:03,833 --> 00:02:09,341
In MKS, one newton is one kilogram,
times one metre per second squared.

47
00:02:10,091 --> 00:02:10,997
Step two.

48
00:02:10,997 --> 00:02:16,256
In CGS, the force unit is the dyne:
one gram times one centimetre per

49
00:02:16,256 --> 00:02:17,493
second squared.

50
00:02:18,243 --> 00:02:19,240
Step three.

51
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:22,428
A gram is ten to the
minus three kilograms.

52
00:02:22,428 --> 00:02:25,670
A centimetre is ten to
the minus two metres.

53
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:27,320
Multiply.

54
00:02:27,299 --> 00:02:30,640
One dyne is ten to the minus five newtons.

55
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:33,264
So one newton is one lakh dynes.

56
00:02:34,014 --> 00:02:35,099
Remember this.

57
00:02:35,099 --> 00:02:38,037
A system of units is a set of base units.

58
00:02:38,037 --> 00:02:41,311
Change them, and every
derived unit changes too.

59
00:02:42,011 --> 00:02:43,069
Side by side.

60
00:02:43,069 --> 00:02:47,165
One newton is roughly the weight
of a small apple in your hand.

61
00:02:47,565 --> 00:02:51,648
One dyne is a hundred thousand times
smaller, less than the weight of a

62
00:02:51,648 --> 00:02:52,548
mosquito.

63
00:02:52,931 --> 00:02:54,019
Now you try.

64
00:02:54,019 --> 00:02:58,822
Which is bigger, one joule or
one erg, the CGS unit of energy?

65
00:02:58,822 --> 00:03:00,174
Pause and decide.

66
00:03:03,374 --> 00:03:04,274
One joule!

67
00:03:04,265 --> 00:03:06,755
It is ten to the power seven ergs.

68
00:03:06,755 --> 00:03:10,906
Kilograms and metres are much
bigger than grams and centimetres.

69
00:03:11,556 --> 00:03:13,266
Now back to Aarav's tyre.

70
00:03:13,616 --> 00:03:15,940
p s i means pounds per square inch.

71
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:18,395
That is pressure in the British system.

72
00:03:18,795 --> 00:03:20,729
The bar is a metric unit.

73
00:03:20,729 --> 00:03:25,761
One bar is exactly one lakh pascals,
close to the air pressure around us.

74
00:03:26,161 --> 00:03:30,977
One p s i is about six thousand
eight hundred ninety five pascals.

75
00:03:31,377 --> 00:03:34,986
So forty p s i is about
two point seven six bar.

76
00:03:34,986 --> 00:03:38,789
Pump until the gauge reads
just under two point eight.

77
00:03:39,439 --> 00:03:40,346
Your turn.

78
00:03:40,346 --> 00:03:42,129
Is the bar an SI unit?

79
00:03:42,129 --> 00:03:43,464
Pause and decide.

80
00:03:46,664 --> 00:03:47,564
No!

81
00:03:47,108 --> 00:03:49,647
The SI unit of pressure is the pascal.

82
00:03:49,647 --> 00:03:53,538
The bar equals one lakh pascals,
but it is not an SI unit.

83
00:03:54,188 --> 00:03:55,923
Now, one worked example.

84
00:03:56,273 --> 00:04:00,339
Change one joule into ergs,
the CGS unit of energy.

85
00:04:00,839 --> 00:04:04,371
A joule is one kilogram metre
squared per second squared.

86
00:04:05,121 --> 00:04:06,815
Put in CGS units.

87
00:04:06,815 --> 00:04:09,268
One kilogram is a thousand grams.

88
00:04:09,268 --> 00:04:12,882
One square metre is ten
thousand square centimetres.

89
00:04:13,632 --> 00:04:14,532
Multiply.

90
00:04:14,456 --> 00:04:17,797
One thousand times ten
thousand is ten million.

91
00:04:18,547 --> 00:04:21,746
So one joule equals ten
to the power seven ergs.

92
00:04:22,396 --> 00:04:23,862
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:24,212 --> 00:04:29,204
Many students write one newton equals
one thousand dynes, because a kilogram

94
00:04:29,204 --> 00:04:30,904
is one thousand grams.

95
00:04:31,254 --> 00:04:33,128
But the metre changes too.

96
00:04:33,128 --> 00:04:35,317
Kilogram to gram is a thousand.

97
00:04:35,317 --> 00:04:37,608
Metre to centimetre is a hundred.

98
00:04:37,608 --> 00:04:41,205
Together, one newton is ten
to the power five dynes.

99
00:04:41,855 --> 00:04:43,306
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:43,306 --> 00:04:48,262
As short questions: convert between
systems, or find a unit in a new system.

101
00:04:48,863 --> 00:04:50,103
Exam level now.

102
00:04:50,103 --> 00:04:55,267
A new system has a mass unit of one
gram, a length unit of one centimetre,

103
00:04:55,267 --> 00:04:58,253
and a time unit of zero point one second.

104
00:04:58,253 --> 00:05:01,177
How many new force units make one newton?

105
00:05:01,177 --> 00:05:02,470
Pause and solve.

106
00:05:07,870 --> 00:05:09,134
The answer is B.

107
00:05:09,134 --> 00:05:10,800
Ten to the power three.

108
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:16,326
The new force unit is one gram times
one centimetre, divided by zero point

109
00:05:16,326 --> 00:05:17,805
one second squared.

110
00:05:18,305 --> 00:05:22,806
That is ten to the minus three newtons,
so one newton is a thousand new

111
00:05:22,806 --> 00:05:23,706
units.

112
00:05:23,403 --> 00:05:25,868
C forgets that the time unit changed.

113
00:05:26,468 --> 00:05:27,931
Here is your recap.

114
00:05:27,931 --> 00:05:29,434
Screenshot this card.

115
00:05:32,634 --> 00:05:35,470
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

116
00:05:35,870 --> 00:05:36,770
One.

117
00:05:36,475 --> 00:05:40,316
What do CGS, FPS and MKS stand for?

118
00:05:41,216 --> 00:05:42,116
Two.

119
00:05:41,730 --> 00:05:43,908
How many dynes make one newton?

120
00:05:44,808 --> 00:05:45,708
Three.

121
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:48,612
Is forty p s i more or
less than three bar?

122
00:05:49,712 --> 00:05:53,348
Next time: Aarav rides at
eighteen kilometres per hour.

123
00:05:53,348 --> 00:05:55,765
How many metres per second is that?

124
00:05:55,765 --> 00:05:57,973
We find out in unit conversions.
